// The NDK, layer one: the Amiga custom chip as exile declarations.
//
// This is a LIBRARY, not a binding. It states what the silicon is - addresses,
// widths, directions, and which registers form an indivisible set - and emits
// nothing. Every construct here is compile-time: a `sigil` names a byte range
// and who may touch it, a `ward` names the layout over a base, and an `atomic`
// group names registers whose accesses must lie in one seal region. A program
// that uses none of them pays nothing; a program that uses them pays the same
// stores it would have written by hand.
//
// It binds to SILICON, never to an OS. There is no exec, no library base, no
// OpenLibrary: an address here is a fact about the chip, so the same file serves
// a program running under AmigaOS and one running on bare metal.
//
// WHERE THIS LIVES is not a design choice yet - it is a constraint. The loader
// resolves `use ndk::*` relative to the IMPORTING file's directory and has no
// library search path, so the library has to sit beside its consumer. When a
// search path lands, this directory moves and nothing else changes.

// ---- ownership regions ------------------------------------------------------
// A sigil is a claim, not an address: exactly one module may `own` each, and a
// field access from a non-owner is refused. The ranges are the HRM's, byte
// addresses inclusive.
//
// MEASURED, and a library user has to know it: a sigil with NO claimant enforces
// nothing. Shipping the declaration hands out the resource's NAME and RANGE; the
// protection begins when some module says `own`. That is coherent - there is no
// trespass on unowned ground - but it means this file alone protects nothing, and
// a program that wants the guarantee has to claim what it drives.
pub sigil Blitter { 0xDFF040 .. 0xDFF067 }
pub sigil Serial  { 0xDFF018 .. 0xDFF019, 0xDFF030 .. 0xDFF033 }
pub sigil DmaCtl  { 0xDFF002 .. 0xDFF003, 0xDFF096 .. 0xDFF097 }
pub sigil IntCtl  { 0xDFF01C .. 0xDFF01F, 0xDFF09A .. 0xDFF09D }

// ---- the control registers --------------------------------------------------
// DMACON and INTENA are SET/CLR registers: bit 15 decides whether the other bits
// are set or cleared, and the live state is readable only from the SEPARATE
// read port. That is exactly the shape a seal exists for, and the reason each
// pair is declared atomic: the read and the write that depends on it must not be
// torn apart by an interrupt.
pub ward Control {
    dmaconr: u16 at 0x002 read;
    intenar: u16 at 0x01C read;
    intreqr: u16 at 0x01E read;
    dmacon:  u16 at 0x096 write;
    intena:  u16 at 0x09A write;
    intreq:  u16 at 0x09C write;

    atomic { dmaconr, dmacon }
    atomic { intenar, intena }
}

// ---- the blitter ------------------------------------------------------------
// BLTSIZE is the write that STARTS the hardware, so every pointer, modulo and
// control word it will use must already be in place. The group says they belong
// together; it does NOT say BLTSIZE goes last, which is a stronger property and
// a separate declaration if a consumer ever needs it.
pub ward Blit {
    bltcon0: u16 at 0x040 write;
    bltcon1: u16 at 0x042 write;
    bltafwm: u16 at 0x044 write;
    bltalwm: u16 at 0x046 write;
    bltcpt:  u32 at 0x048 write;
    bltbpt:  u32 at 0x04C write;
    bltapt:  u32 at 0x050 write;
    bltdpt:  u32 at 0x054 write;
    bltsize: u16 at 0x058 write;
    bltcmod: u16 at 0x060 write;
    bltbmod: u16 at 0x062 write;
    bltamod: u16 at 0x064 write;
    bltdmod: u16 at 0x066 write;

    atomic { bltcon0, bltcon1, bltafwm, bltalwm, bltcpt, bltbpt, bltapt, bltdpt,
             bltsize, bltcmod, bltbmod, bltamod, bltdmod }
}

// ---- the serial line --------------------------------------------------------
// The console a bare Amiga has in silicon, and the one runtime/sys_bare.c writes
// through. SERDATR carries status (transmit-buffer-empty) as well as data.
pub ward Ser {
    serdatr: u16 at 0x018 read;
    serdat:  u16 at 0x030 write;
    serper:  u16 at 0x032 write;
}

// ---- the canonical instances ------------------------------------------------
// The overlay at the real base, for programs that talk to the chip. They cost
// nothing: a ward instance has zero storage and each field access folds its base
// at the use site. A test that wants to RUN the idiom on a host declares its own
// instance over a byte array instead - the ward TYPE is the reusable part, and
// the base is the consumer's to choose.
pub ward control: Control at 0xDFF000;
pub ward blitter: Blit    at 0xDFF000;
pub ward serial:  Ser     at 0xDFF000;
